# 27. Spring Boot Persistence Architecture

Database access follows a layered backend architecture.

                    Controller
                        │
                        ▼
                      DTO
                        │
                        ▼
                     Service
                        │
                        ▼
                   Repository
                        │
                        ▼
                  JPA / Hibernate
                        │
                        ▼
                    PostgreSQL

Each layer has a distinct responsibility.

## Controller

Handles HTTP requests and responses.

It should not contain database logic.

## DTO

Defines data entering or leaving the API.

Entities should not automatically be exposed directly to frontend clients.

## Service

Contains business rules.

Examples include:

- Score calculation.
- Team validation.
- Reset operations.
- Soft deletion.
- Event state validation.

## Repository

Provides persistence operations using Spring Data JPA.

## Entity

Maps the Java domain/persistence model to PostgreSQL tables.

---

# 28. Spring Data JPA

The backend will use Spring Data JPA repositories.

Conceptually:

    EventRepository
    EventSettingsRepository
    TeamRepository
    TeamMemberRepository
    UserRepository
    ScoreEventRepository

Repositories handle persistence concerns.

Business logic must remain in services rather than being placed directly inside controllers.

---

# 29. Transaction Management

Operations that modify related records must be transactional.

Examples include:

- Awarding points.
- Deducting points.
- Custom score adjustments.
- Resetting all scores.
- Creating a Team with its members.
- Updating Team membership.

Spring's transaction management will be used at the service layer.

Conceptually:

    Controller
        |
        v
    ScoreService
        |
        | Transaction begins
        |
        +--> Update Team
        |
        +--> Save ScoreEvent
        |
        | Commit
        v
    Database

If saving the ScoreEvent fails, the Team score update must also fail.

The database must never contain a score change without its corresponding history record.

---

# 30. Concurrency

The application is intended to support multiple devices simultaneously.

Examples include:

- Administrator PC.
- Administrator tablet.
- Mobile phone.
- Public scoreboard.
- Television display.

This creates the possibility of simultaneous score updates.

The backend must therefore treat PostgreSQL as the authoritative source of score state.

The service should retrieve and update current state transactionally rather than trusting a score value supplied by a client.

As the system develops, concurrency control may use:

- JPA optimistic locking.
- A version column.
- Transaction isolation.
- Database locking where specifically necessary.

Optimistic locking is the preferred initial approach for normal administrative operations.

---

# 31. Authentication

Administrative API operations must require authentication.

The target authentication flow is:

    Administrator
         |
         v
    Login Request
         |
         v
    Spring Security
         |
         v
    Credential Validation
         |
         v
       JWT
         |
         v
    Authenticated API Requests

Passwords are stored only as secure hashes.

JWT tokens must not contain passwords or sensitive credentials.

---

# 32. Authorisation

The application should distinguish between public and protected operations.

Public operations may include:

- View leaderboard.
- View active event.
- View registration information.

Protected operations include:

- Add Team.
- Edit Team.
- Delete Team.
- Award points.
- Deduct points.
- Custom score adjustment.
- Reset scores.
- Change event settings.
- Manage administrators.

Spring Security will enforce these permissions.

---

# 33. Database Security

Security controls include:

- Password hashing.
- Parameterised queries through JPA.
- Restricted database credentials.
- Environment-based configuration.
- Authentication.
- Role-based authorisation.
- Input validation.
- Audit records.
- HTTPS in production.

Database credentials must never be committed to GitHub.

Production credentials should be supplied using environment variables or a secure secret-management mechanism.

---

# 34. API and Database Separation

The React application must never contain PostgreSQL credentials.

The architecture is:

    React
       |
       | HTTPS
       v
    Spring Boot REST API
       |
       | JDBC / JPA
       v
    PostgreSQL

and never:

    React
       |
       X
       |
    PostgreSQL

This ensures database access remains controlled by the backend.

---

# 35. Responsive Client Support

Database design is independent of screen size.

The same backend API serves:

- Television displays.
- Desktop computers.
- Laptops.
- Tablets.
- Mobile phones.

The React frontend is responsible for responsive presentation.

The backend provides consistent JSON resources regardless of the requesting device.

This allows multiple device types to display the same authoritative event state.

---

# 36. Real-Time Updates

The original Firebase implementation provided native real-time synchronisation.

PostgreSQL itself does not replace the client synchronisation behaviour provided by Firebase.

The rebuilt architecture separates persistence from real-time communication.

Initial versions may use normal REST API refreshes.

The architecture can later introduce:

- WebSockets.
- Server-Sent Events.
- Spring WebSocket.
- STOMP messaging.

A future real-time architecture could be:

                    PostgreSQL
                         │
                         ▼
                    Spring Boot
                         │
                    WebSocket
                         │
              ┌──────────┼──────────┐
              ▼          ▼          ▼
             TV         Phone       PC

A score update performed on an administrator device could therefore immediately update connected leaderboard displays.

